OBP: how the GNU/Linux build of openboot.bin is checked against Sun's
======================================================================

Nothing here is "looks the same". Every line below is a cmp, a run, or a
boot, with the measured numbers kept. Where a difference remains it is named,
explained and shown not to matter -- never waved away.

The gate is seven scripts under tests/full/obp/internal/; run it rather than
trusting a status line in a file:

    make obp-verify                 # no tarball, no Solaris host

tests/full/obp/README maps the directory. This document is the argument: what is
claimed (1), against what (2), by which tests (3, 4), what still differs and
why none of it is a defect (5), everything the GNU path does differently (6),
what is NOT covered (7), and how to re-run any of it (8). Appendix A keeps
the history -- how byte identity was reached in the first place, against a
Solaris build of our own, before Sun's own shipped images became the
reference.


1. The claim
------------

openboot.bin built on GNU/Linux with build/obp/gnu/ is equivalent to the one
Sun's own toolchain builds on Solaris from the same source. "Equivalent" is
three separate claims, each proven by its own method:

  BYTE-IDENTICAL   The same bytes. Holds for every dropin record of the two
                   images Sun itself built and shipped -- niagara2/release
                   (Sun's 2007 build) and niagara/t1_fpga (Sun's 2009 image)
                   -- rebuilt here from pristine source (sun-records); for
                   every kernel dictionary bootstrapped from Sun's root
                   (dic-kernels); and, against a Solaris build of our own,
                   for every dropin and -- with the C compiler taken out of
                   the comparison -- the reset record too (Appendix A).

  FUNCTIONALLY     The same function, proven by running it. Holds for the one
  IDENTICAL        piece of C compiled differently by design, decomp.c: both
                   compilers' objects decompress every real payload in the
                   image to identical output (decomp-ab).

  FUNCTIONALLY     The same behaviour, proven by booting. Both machine
  EQUIVALENT       configurations reach the Solaris login prompt on the
                   GNU-built image (the boots, section 4).

What differs after all that is five named classes, all measured, none of them
changing what the PROM does: two never reach the image at all, one is dead
the moment the PROM re-initialises at cold start, one is a build stamp that
is meant to differ, and the last is the same code from another compiler --
run and compared output for output. Section 5.

No file under src/obp is edited on either path -- both build in a copy and
apply patches there. Every difference between the two builds is in the build
layer, inventoried in full in section 6.


