             build/obp/gnu/build-obp.sh <platform>/<target>
         (niagara/t1_fpga = legion, niagara1-hw/release = pci)
Pristine: add OBP_PATCHES= to build Sun's source with none of our patches.
Mixed:   add REFERENCE_DIR=<a reference release dir> to take
         decomp.o/version.fth/revlevel from the reference and keep everything
         else GNU. Used by Appendix A, and by sun-records for the stamp.


3. The gate: make obp-verify
-----------------------------

Seven scripts, tests/full/obp/internal/*.sh, run in this order by
tests/full/obp/run (`make obp-verify` and `make obp-test` are the same thing).
Each is independently runnable; `run` shares one pci build across those that
need it (lib/build-pci.sh), so the whole gate is one build, not four. Order is
by dependency: a later test's claim rests on an earlier one, so `run` stops at
the first failure rather than repeating it under another name.

src-intact
    PRISTINE-MANIFEST's sha256 for every vendored file matches the working
    tree. No QEMU, no build; one sha256sum -c pass over every file of the
    manifest.
    It proves only internal consistency -- that the tree is what this
    repository claims was imported. Whether that claim is honest is
    src-pristine's job (section 4).
    Found on its first run: a file edited after import without the manifest
    being regenerated. Fixed.

dic-kernels
    The three kernel dictionaries a pci build bootstraps from forth.dic --
    kernel-xref, k32t32-xref, k64t32 -- equal the copies Sun shipped in
    src/obp byte for byte, and the six word listings (.fth, .headless.fth)
    match line for line below the copyright banner Sun's 2009 packaging
    prepended to generated files too.
    These need no reconciliation at all, unlike the host dictionaries below:
    they are pure kernel saves, with no live user area and the same origin as
    Sun's.

dic-fixpoint
    The chain is a fixed point, on this engine and not only on Sun's.
    Every build already asserts the first-generation fixpoint as a build-time
    check inside bootstrap-dics.sh (kernel-xref == k32t32-xref: the 32-bit
    kernel is metacompiled twice, once by the shipped root and once by a host
    rebuilt around the new kernel, and the two must agree -- the property
    Sun's own depend.mk:177-180 relies on when it offers the -xref models as
    forth.dic replacements). This test adds the second generation: bootstrap
    once from Sun's root (gen1), then bootstrap again using gen1's own
    forth32-xref.dic AS the root (gen2).
    Measured: gen2 reproduces that root byte for byte, and all twelve
    artefacts (six dictionaries, six listings) are identical between gen1 and
    gen2. Previously measured only on the Solaris host with Sun's native
    engine (section 4, the Solaris bootstrap); this is the same result on
    the cross-built one.

dic-host
    The HOST dictionaries -- forth32-xref.dic and f32t32.dic, the ones this
    build actually runs rather than merely metacompiles -- against Sun's
    shipped copies. These cannot be byte-identical, for two independent and
    already-understood reasons:
      - Sun's shipped copies were saved WITHOUT -D SUN4V and therefore lack
        one assembler word, rdver ([ifndef] SUN4V, cpu/sparc/assem.fth:386-388);
        Sun's own sun4v build recipe passes -D SUN4V to these steps
        (PLATFLAGS, niagara1-hw/release/Makefile:55) and so does
        bootstrap-dics.sh. That word's absence shortens the text by 20 bytes
        and shifts everything after it.
      - They are live-run saves, so they also carry this engine's own
        addresses: the engine-binary-address class (section 5).
    lib/dic-diff.py separates both from real content differences. It compares
    the body as 4-byte cells, locates every length-changing region, and for
    each remaining differing cell tries the entry-delta correction, the shift
    in bytes (a stored dictionary address whose target moved), and the shift
    in instruction words (a SPARC CALL's displacement counts instructions,
    not bytes, so a call site and target on opposite sides of the cut differ
    by shift/4). What is left in the data/user-area region it reports
    separately as the accepted class, not as a failure.
    Measured on both dictionaries: tlen differs by exactly -20 B; every
    differing cell in the text region is explained by those corrections (byte
    shifts, call-word shifts, entry-delta, the global shift), and the rest
    fall in the user-area tail. Nothing is left unexplained in the text
    region. The counts are printed by the test.
    tokenize.dic is deliberately NOT compared this way. It carries the same
    rdver shift plus several smaller ones from loadtok.fth's own
    conditionals, close enough together that a single global shift constant
    does not separate them. Its correctness rests on what it produces
    instead -- every tokenized dropin byte-identical to Sun's (Appendix A) --
    which is exactly the principle section 5 states for any saved image.

sun-records
    Pristine builds (OBP_PATCHES=, none of our patches) of the two targets Sun
    shipped an image of, walked record by record against
    tests/full/obp/expected/sun-records.sha256: the same record names in the
    same order, and every record's sha256 equal to Sun's.
    Every record is hashed and identical except OBP and bootprom in each
    image, which are compared by name and position only, for the reasons in
    section 5 (a different C compiler; the engine-binary-address class; the
    build stamp).
    The expected file is committed, so this needs no tarball. Regenerating it
    does: external/sun-records-regenerate.sh, section 4.

patch-locality
    This project's four patches/obp/forth/*.patch change only what they say
    they change. Three of them touch device drivers that each become their
    own dropin -- obp/dev/ide/pci and obp/dev/network/ophir, i.e. the records
    named class010100 and ophir in the pci configuration. The fourth,
    md-iodevice-reg-guard.patch, touches arch/sun4v/md-iodevice-props.fth,
    core Forth source that ends up inside the bootprom record.
    A pristine pci build and the patched (product) build are therefore
    required to differ in exactly four records -- ophir, class010100,
    and the two that are unscored anyway (OBP, bootprom) -- and the two
    device dropins must ACTUALLY differ, or the patches did nothing and
    "identical everywhere" would be a false pass. Measured: every other
    record byte-identical, the four expected ones differing.
    Two things this test turned up on its first run:
      - The pci chain carries two DISTINCT records both named "support-pkg"
        (Sun's own dropins.src binds that package into more than one
        subtree). lib/records.py therefore compares by chain position, never
        by name; a name-keyed map would have silently dropped one.
      - The OBP record embeds version.fth's stamp in its header: section 5,
        the build-stamp class.
    What this test cannot show is the locality of md-iodevice-reg-guard.patch
    itself, since its effect lands inside bootprom, which is unscored for
    unrelated reasons. Stated as a limit, not assumed away.

dict-content
    The pci build's stand.dic word list -- every word in every vocabulary,
    via the dictionary's own `words` (lib/dict-dump.sh) -- against
    tests/full/obp/expected/dict-dump.sha256.
    This is a regression check on our own output, not a comparison with Sun:
    a saved image's byte hash is useless as a criterion (section 5) but the
    set of word NAMES does not move on a harmless recompile. The stronger
    claims are sun-records and patch-locality; this is a cheap third check on
    the same ground.

Checked to fail: corrupting one hash in expected/sun-records.sha256 produces
FAIL with the diff, not a silent pass.


4. Evidence outside the gate
-----------------------------

Measurements the gate does not repeat, because each needs something the gate
deliberately does not require: the OpenSPARC tarball, a Solaris host, a boot,
or a second emulator. Each is re-runnable with the resource it names.

src-pristine                                    needs the tarball
    Every file PRISTINE-MANIFEST records really is what Sun shipped, hashes
    re-derived from OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2 rather than taken on trust
    from the manifest. This is the half of provenance src-intact cannot do.
    Measured: every file of the manifest matches exactly (the hypervisor and
    obp members of the archive). One pass over the whole bzip2 archive.
        make obp-test-external TARBALL=<tarball>

sun-records-regenerate                          needs the tarball
    Rewrites tests/full/obp/expected/*.sha256 from Sun's own two shipped images
    and a fresh build. Run it after a change to src/obp, and review the diff
    before committing -- regenerating silently accepts whatever the tree
    currently produces, which is only right if that tree is known good.
    Checked: run with no source change, it reproduces both committed files
    byte for byte.

decomp-ab: the one C file compiled differently, run rather than read
    Isolates decomp.c, compiled by Sun cc in one build and GCC in the other.
    lib/decomp-ab/decomp-ab.sh links a freestanding harness (no libc, raw
    ta 0x10 syscalls) against each object in turn and runs it under
    qemu-sparc32plus on every COMP record's payload, calling
    decompress(payload+0x10, comp_size, dest) exactly as
    arch/sun/reset-dropin.fth:131-153 does.
    Measured: 28 of 28 payloads decompress identically between the two
    objects; 28 of 28 output lengths match the header's uncompressed size;
    26 of 28 match the original uncompressed source byte for byte (font and
    usbkbds have no surviving source -- Sun's own recipe deletes the .fnt
    after makedi). Payloads from 512 B (sun-logo.dat) to 419,616 B
    (bootprom.bin). Object sizes differ as expected: GCC .text 968 B against
    Sun's 1,268 B.

A second, independent emulator
    Isolates our own qemu-sparc64 build: the same mixed build run under
    Ubuntu's qemu-user-static 8.2.2 and under a clean-room build of QEMU from
    source.
    Measured: every artefact identical, stand.dic and bootprom.bin included.
    The guest brk placement for a static sparc64 binary comes from the binary,
    not from the emulator, so the emulator cannot leak into a saved image.
    Stock qemu-user-static is a valid QEMU_SPARC64.

The boots
    The current evidence is the login gate (tests/full/login, run by make
    test-all): legion booted to Solaris's login prompt from a clean commit,
    in three containers and on the host. pci is booted by hand
    (build_matrix, section 8).

    The first boots of this build, 2026-09-29, from an uncommitted tree
    (banner "git <rev>-dirty") -- dictionaries bootstrapped from forth.dic,
    32-bit tokenizer -- both configs from one firmware set:
    pci: boots to the Solaris 10 login prompt (SunOS 5.10 Generic_147147-26).
   https://unix0cc.github.io/md-artefacts/obp-gnu32-pci-4096__s10u11_fast.txt
    legion: boots to the login prompt (SunOS 5.10 Generic_118822-23).
   https://unix0cc.github.io/md-artefacts/obp-gnu32-legion-4096__disk.s10hw2.txt
    The earlier runs, before the dictionary bootstrap, with the same result:
   https://unix0cc.github.io/md-artefacts/obp-gnu-pci-4096__s10u11_fast.txt
   https://unix0cc.github.io/md-artefacts/obp-gnu-legion-4096__disk.s10hw2.txt

    The pci boot needs a QEMU that gives each PCI slot its own interrupt
    line (INTx swizzled by slot). md/pci's interrupt map routes each slot to
    its own ino; a QEMU that routes every slot's INTA to one ino delivers
    the mpt controller's interrupts to the NIC's ino instead. Seen on
    2026-09-29: the same firmware under such a build reached the kernel and
    then looped on mpt "Disconnected command timeout"; under a build with
    the swizzle it booted. That build differs in other patches too, so the
    mechanism is read from the code, not isolated or observed in the guest.
    The same symptom from an ad-hoc launch line in the first pci run was
    recorded then as launch configuration, not firmware.

Build from clean, repeatedly, and by someone else
    rm -rf OUTDIR, build, verify the dropin chain. Built clean three times in
    scratch and then in a fresh clone by someone else, chain verified each time
    (for pci and for legion, ending exactly at EOF).

The Solaris bootstrap: every shipped .dic but one is a product of this source
    The question this answers: are the seven .dic files in src/obp opaque
    blobs, or does the source in this tree produce them? Run on the Solaris host
    with Sun's own toolchain and native engine, one full sun-path build per
    generation (15-36 min each), with dictionaries deleted from src/obp
    beforehand:
        gen1   Sun's root, all seven .dic present
        gen1b  Sun's root, the five intermediates deleted
        gen2   root := gen1b's own forth32-xref.dic, five deleted
        gen1t  Sun's root, the five AND tokenize.dic deleted -- forth.dic the
               only .dic left in the tree
        gen1m  as gen1, from a fresh git clone (checkout-time mtimes,
               restore-mtimes.sh not run)
    Measured, sha256 prefix of every dictionary the build writes:

        dictionary                shipped   gen1 / 1b / 2 / 1t / 1m
        ------------------------  --------  ------------------------
        kernel-xref, k32t32-xref  405a81d3  405a81d3   (all five)
        k64t32                    b1bf6067  b1bf6067   (all five)
        forth32-xref              2820745e  53c3f5a7   (gen2: equals its root)
        f32t32                    3f9095e6  b44534d1
        tokenize                  db1583ec  2cbbcc93

    On the GNU path the same files compare as follows (dic-kernels and
    dic-host, section 3): the three kernel dictionaries equal the shipped
    ones; the three host dictionaries (forth32-xref, f32t32, tokenize) differ
    by design, as they do on Solaris. dic-host checks the first two cell by
    cell; tokenize.dic is not compared that way, its correctness rests on the
    dropins it produces (Appendix A).

    So: the kernels rebuild from source equal to Sun's shipped files bit for
    bit; the fixpoint is reached in one generation; the sun path needs
    exactly one binary, forth.dic (gen1t), and does not depend on file mtimes
    (gen1m) -- every rule lists the engine, which the build always compiles
    fresh. Of 98 files in the release directory, 87 are identical between
    generations; the other 11 carry either the build stamp or the build
    directory's own path -- Sun's cc embeds it in its objects, which is why
    two generations built in differently named directories land one byte
    apart.
    The shipped HOST dictionaries differ from the rebuilt ones by 20 bytes:
    the single assembler word rdver, present only when -D SUN4V is absent.
    Cutting those 20 bytes leaves, in f32t32, only pointers past the cut and
    calls across it -- nothing unexplained; in tokenize, the same plus two
    words of alignment padding. rdver never reaches a kernel, and on sun4v it
    names an instruction that traps: "access to the VER register is now
    hyperprivileged (and VER was renamed the HVER register)" (UltraSPARC
    Architecture 2005, p. 13). dic-host now checks the Linux side of this
    same comparison on every gate run.


5. What still differs, and why none of it is a defect
------------------------------------------------------

Five classes. Each is named here because the rest of this repository refers
to them by name -- in test scripts, in build rules, and in the comments that
explain why a given file is compared one way and not another.

5.1 The compiler class -- decomp.o
    Sun cc -fast -xarch=v8 and gcc -m32 -mcpu=v8 -O2 emit different code for
    the same 317-line C file. Taken out of the comparison entirely by the
    mixed build (Appendix A: reset.bin identical once the reference's own
    decomp.o is linked in) and shown harmless by running it (decomp-ab:
    identical behaviour on every real payload).

5.2 The engine-binary-address class -- stand.dic, bootprom.bin, any live save
    A saved Forth image contains the dictionary origin (header offset 20) and
    pointers into the engine's own C .data/.bss. These depend on which binary
    saved the image -- a GCC-built static engine and a Sun-built one have
    different heap starts and different data layouts -- and on nothing else:
    two different emulators produce identical images (section 4). The loader
    rebases origin-relative pointers and the engine re-establishes its own at
    startup, which is why an image saved by either engine runs under the
    other.
    Measured, for bootprom.bin's 181 differing bytes: 6 bytes are three
    32-bit dictionary addresses in the body (origin plus identical offsets);
    175 bytes are 50 cells of the saved Forth user area in the 0x1960-byte
    tail -- 22 dictionary pointers (`here` among them, all at the one origin
    delta), 27 pointers into the engine binary's own .data/.bss, and one
    2-byte residual: a user-area counter at +0x2b8 holding 0xb7f against
    0xb7d, not an address, still unidentified by name. The PROM re-initialises
    the user area at cold start; every host pointer in it is dead on arrival
    in either build.
    Consequence, and it is a rule: never use a .dic, stand.dic or bootprom.bin
    hash as a pass/fail criterion. Compare what they produce, or run them.
    dic-host is the one place a saved image IS compared, and only because
    dic-diff.py can separate this class out cell by cell.

5.3 The symbol-value class -- reset.o
    The st_value of the `reset` symbol in .symtab is a runtime address; it
    differs while the relocation tables are identical, and the mixed build
    shows it does not reach reset.bin.

5.4 The build-stamp class -- version.fth, revlevel, and the OBP record
    Sun's build stamps the date, the logname and a COMMENT into version.fth
    and increments revlevel; ours stamps build/stamp.sh's date
    (SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, else the clean commit's, in UTC) and the git
    revision, with revlevel 0. By design, and the reason REFERENCE_DIR exists: a
    comparison run copies Sun's whole stamp rather than pretending the dates
    match.
    The stamp is not confined to version.fth. The OBP record's 16-byte name
    field carries a build timestamp after the name, and the format decodes
    cleanly on all three images at hand:

      name[16] = "OBP\0\0\0" <year:2> <month> <day> <hour> <minute> 04 00 00 7f

        4f425000000007d70b070e020400007f  Sun niagara2/release  2007/11/07 14:02
        4f425000000007d903120d320400007f  Sun niagara/t1_fpga   2009/03/18 13:50
        4f425000000007ea091a16190400007f  a build of ours       2026/09/26 22:25

    Sun's two decode to exactly the dates their own banner strings carry, and
    ours to the minute of its version.fth stamp -- same layout, same
    trailing four bytes. The date is version.fth's own compile-signature,
    written into the header by save-obj (arch/sun/reset/common.fth:133),
    and the four bytes after it are major/minor/patch-release# parsed from
    obp-release, then 7f. An earlier note here said the dropin builder took
    this date from the clock independently of version.fth; that was wrong.
    Measured 2026-09-29: two builds a minute apart with one
    SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH give byte-identical openboot.bin (and q.bin, reset.bin,
    MDs, nvram1), and the record reads 07ea 09 1c 17 06 = the epoch's
    2026/09/28 23:06, not the build time.
    Two builds of different commits still differ here, patched or not:
    patch-locality sees this, and it is one more reason (beyond the compiler
    class) that OBP is compared by name and position rather than by hash.

5.5 The path class -- dropins.mk
    Path spelling, and one buildoptions line rewritten from dmake to GNU make
    syntax. Neither makeprom nor makedi reads this file; every dependency
    edge in it is the same (Appendix A).


